    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good point about the SLAs and seals. I suppose my other concern is the \"unseen\" risks. In these types of high-value, high-stakes logistics, how do we guard against small, unpredictable disruptions or \"nuisance\" failures—things that aren't necessarily major theft, but just little errors or environmental factors that creep in unnoticed during the handoff? Is there a way to account for those kinds of \"hidden\" threats?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Unseen\" risks, the little things that derail a perfect plan, are often the most insidious, like a misplaced piece of dry ice or a slightly faulty sensor reading. You can guard against these \"nuisance\" failures by implementing a multi-layered redundancy system, which is much more robust than relying on a single piece of equipment. For example, don't just rely on the primary temperature logger; use secondary, independent monitoring devices placed strategically within the packaging. Furthermore, mandate a rigorous pre-departure and pre-arrival inspection checklist that goes beyond just checking the seals—it should include verifying the operational status of the cooling unit itself and confirming the integrity of the insulation materials. This proactive, granular checking is key to catching those tiny errors before they become catastrophic excursions.",
      "source": "target",
